# 012 — 327 translated strings were silently discarded by duplicate JSON keys.
#
# 28 of the admin locale files (plus $schema.json) declared `commissions`,
# `scheduledClosure` or `views` TWICE at the same level. JSON has no merge
# semantics — the last occurrence wins outright — so the earlier block was
# dropped at parse time and those keys fell back to English for every non-English
# user. In de.json, for example, `commissions` at line 13 held the whole
# `rulesTable` sub-tree and was overwritten by the block at line 4012.
#
# The duplicate blocks are DISJOINT: there is not one leaf where the two
# definitions disagree. So this is a pure merge, verified as such:
#
#   strings restored ........ 327
#   strings lost ............ 0
#   visible values changed .. 0
#
# Each merged key is re-inserted at the LAST occurrence's position — the one
# that wins today — so the patch moves the small orphaned block down rather than
# reordering the file, and stays reviewable.
#
# Files are rewritten with `json.dumps(indent=2, ensure_ascii=False)`, which
# round-trips a duplicate-free file byte-for-byte, so the only changes here are
# the merges themselves.
#
# This does NOT fix `validate-translations.spec.ts`, which fails on a pristine
# tree for an unrelated reason: en.json carries 16 `sellers.*` keys that
# $schema.json does not declare. Separate concern, separate patch.

